WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:05.160
Space is often referred to as “the final
frontier”.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:11.019
As planet Earth grows crowded, with few blank
places left on the map to explore, mankind

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:16.350
has turned its attention to the stars, seeking
to uncover the secrets of the Universe.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:20.810
With so much space to cover, exciting new
discoveries are made almost daily, and as

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:24.010
technology progresses we’re seeing farther
than ever before.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:31.891
Let’s take a look at 9 Fantastic Things
in Space that will blow your brain into the

00:00:31.891 --> 00:00:34.750
cosmos.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.580
Floating among the other space rocks in our
solar system’s asteroid belt, the asteroid

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:45.730
designated P/2013 P5 doesn’t quite fit in
with the rest.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.589
Most asteroids are simply pinpoints of light
against the black of space through our telescopes,

00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:55.930
but this one in particular drew the attention
of NASA scientists due to the six milky white

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.930
tails that seem to sprout from it.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:04.110
It’s not every day you find a “lawn sprinkler”
in space, so naturally, astronomers wanted

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.600
to figure out what caused it.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:13.360
The tails seemed to change every few days—quite
perplexing until they realized that the asteroid

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.430
was actually spinning quite fast.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.160
Then, things started to click into place.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.140
The prevailing theory regarding the breakdown
of small asteroids into space dust considers

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.400
interstellar collisions to be the most likely
culprit.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:35.180
But since P/2013 P5 lacks a large burst of
stellar dust seemingly cast off all at once,

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.950
the implication is that it’s unique tails
weren’t caused by a collision.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:46.000
Instead, observations offer up another potential
cause—rotational stresses that break up

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.950
asteroids bit by bit.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:54.270
The core of the asteroid is 1,400 feet wide—a
good deal larger than the amount of dust spun

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.230
off to form the tails.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:00.740
As it continued to shed its outer layers,
adding to the several hundred tonnes of dust

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.540
already streaming behind it, scientists will
study it to gather more data on the final

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.550
phase of an asteroid’s life cycle.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:15.810
And hopefully, P/2013 P5 will continue to
water celestial lawns for many years to come.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:21.410
In February of 2006, astronomers searching
for the telltale flash of supernovae via the

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:26.970
Hubble Telescope spotted a bright burst of
light from the constellation Bootes.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:33.240
Mysteriously, nothing was visible at the location
before it flared, and nothing has been visible

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.480
since.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:40.870
After bestowing the phenomena with the name
SCP 06F6, scientists set to work trying to

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.660
reason out what exactly they had witnessed.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:50.390
They dubbed it “an unusual optical transient”
and “one of a new class”—which can be

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:53.780
roughly translated as “we have no idea what
that was”.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:58.849
It’s not likely to have been a supernova—or
at least, not one as we know them—for a

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:00.140
number of reasons.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:06.510
First, a star going supernova brightens dramatically
over a short period of time, before fading

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.040
more slowly than it appeared.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.209
It takes around twenty days on average for
the event to reach its peak intensity.

00:03:13.209 --> 00:03:21.510
But then we have SCP 06F6, which took one
hundred days to reach full luminosity before

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.770
fading away at an equally slow rate.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.550
Slow to arrive and slow to leave—kind of
like in-laws.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:33.180
Second, the supernova explanation takes another
blow when scientists consider the spectral

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.480
lines given off by the event.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:40.459
The light emitted could indicate a carbon-based
star going supernova, which would generate

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:46.420
an explosion of a relatively low temperature
between 5,000 and 6,000 degrees Kelvin—of

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.379
course, this is only low, relative to other
exploding stars.

00:03:50.379 --> 00:03:56.680
However, in August of 2006 the European X-ray
satellite discovered an X-ray glow around

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:03.489
SCP 06F6’s location—not a feature shared
with other low temperature supernovae.

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:09.210
Third, the event generated unusual patterns
in the visible light spectrum, showing “broad

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:13.500
line features” in the blue light region
while maintaining “continuous emission”

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.269
in the red light region.

00:04:15.269 --> 00:04:20.099
There is currently no known object that shares
those variations.

00:04:20.099 --> 00:04:24.520
Perhaps there was a containment breach at
some deep-space research facility—that’s

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.900
as likely of a theory as any other, for now.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:33.580
Until we find another “optical transient”
like SCP 06F6 to study, we’ll have to be

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:39.979
comfortable with the knowledge that we understand
far less about the universe than we think.

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:44.879
Another example of how little we actually
know about the furthest reaches of space comes

00:04:44.879 --> 00:04:50.089
in the form of The Great Attractor—proof
that the universe might not be as uniform

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:51.819
as scientists have theorized.

00:04:51.819 --> 00:04:57.770
It’s the great galactic bathtub drain, pulling
in everything around it, and the interesting

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:03.699
part is that there isn’t nearly enough visible
mass to account for the massive gravitational

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:04.699
pull.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:05.699
The Great Attractor manifests itself as a
“dipole pattern on the sky”, first discovered

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:06.699
in the 1970s during a study on the homogeneity
of the Universe.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:09.560
From our perspective in the Milky Way, the
Attractor is located through the “zone of

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:14.729
avoidance”—a region of space filled with
so many stars, galaxies, and other interstellar

00:05:14.729 --> 00:05:19.740
debris that we aren’t able to make out much
using the visible light spectrum.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:25.400
However, its location can be inferred based
on the “great river of galaxies” sixty

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.930
megaparsecs wide—including our own Local
Group, the Virgo Cluster, the Hydra-Centaurus

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:38.039
Supercluster, and more—all flowing toward
the Attractor at a rate of six hundred kilometers

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:39.750
per second.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:44.800
Using infrared and radio based methods of
seeing through the “zone of avoidance”,

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.689
scientists found the Norma Cluster, which
is estimated to contain at least one quadrillion

00:05:49.689 --> 00:05:51.309
solar masses.

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:58.300
That’s astronomically huge, and a good contender
for the source of the Great Attractor—except

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:03.789
that the mass in the Norma Cluster still can’t
sufficiently explain the full range of motion

00:06:03.789 --> 00:06:06.729
astronomers see on a galactic scale.

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:12.699
Located beyond the Norma Cluster is the Shapely
Supercluster, which dwarfs the former at nearly

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:16.749
ten quadrillion solar masses.

00:06:16.749 --> 00:06:21.399
Prevailing theories today suggest that nearby
galactic clusters and even the Great Attractor

00:06:21.399 --> 00:06:28.539
itself are all ultimately moving toward the
staggeringly massive Shapely Supercluster.

00:06:28.539 --> 00:06:32.259
Just when scientists think they’ve found
the biggest thing in the universe, something

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:36.800
else comes along and flips everything we thought
we knew upside down.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.669
Stars have a reputation for being burning
globes of elemental gasses that sparkle at

00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:44.499
night and warm our world during the day.

00:06:44.499 --> 00:06:49.119
But some stars aren’t quite as fiery as
the image you’re led to believe.

00:06:49.119 --> 00:06:54.550
The Y-class “dwarf” stars completely shattered
the stereotype, prompting astronomers to create

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:59.569
a new star classification after their discovery—not
just because of their size, but because they

00:06:59.569 --> 00:07:02.029
were so shockingly cold!

00:07:02.029 --> 00:07:07.409
The first Y dwarf stars were introduced to
the scientific community by NASA in late 2011,

00:07:07.409 --> 00:07:12.539
thanks to the efforts of the Wide-field Infrared
Survey Explorer program.

00:07:12.539 --> 00:07:16.970
They drew a great deal of attention due to
one of their number having an estimated surface

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:22.900
temperature of 25 degrees Celsius—or perhaps
even less!

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:28.439
It’s not often you find stars colder than
the human body out in the cosmos, so this

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:33.650
naturally encouraged the search for additional
Y class stars to see if there were more that

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:35.199
defied expectations.

00:07:35.199 --> 00:07:41.259
In 2014 the search proved fruitful, as another
dwarf star discovered by NASA was found to

00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:48.099
boast sub-zero temperatures of between minus
48 and minus 13 degrees Celsius.

00:07:48.099 --> 00:07:53.520
If there are any planets orbiting it, they’d
better have some good space heaters.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.919
Because these sorts of stars are so abnormal,
it is difficult for scientists to determine

00:07:57.919 --> 00:08:03.270
whether they are in fact remarkably cool stars
or “free-floating planets” drifting through

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.270
the universe.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:08.319
But at the very least, they offer some very
exciting possibilities.

00:08:08.319 --> 00:08:14.620
Swap out our sun for a Y-class dwarf, and
global warming would be nothing but a distant

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:15.620
memory.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:20.119
Sometimes, it isn’t what astronomers find
in space that piques their interest, but what

00:08:20.119 --> 00:08:21.800
they don’t.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:26.610
Current models of the universe allow for a
certain amount of variation, but when there’s

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:32.289
a statistically significant anomaly that breaks
the mold, there are plenty of questions to

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:33.289
be asked.

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:38.979
The discovery of the Eridanus Supervoid begs
one question in particular: why is there a

00:08:38.979 --> 00:08:41.780
massive hole in the Universe?

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:46.610
Scientists study the early years and subsequent
expansion of the universe by mapping the Cosmic

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:50.190
Background Radiation that’s visible in all
directions.

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:54.980
By measuring the variations in energy given
off by radioactive sources, they can identify

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:00.840
pockets of matter like galaxies and superclusters,
as well as the empty spaces in between.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:04.720
On a large scale the universe is expected
to be relatively uniform when it comes to

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.110
the distribution of background radiation.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:13.190
But near the constellation Eridanus, there’s
a peculiar pocket of—nothing.

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:18.620
The Eridanus Supervoid is somewhere between
six and ten billion light years away, and

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:22.370
NASA estimates it to be 500 million light
years across.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:27.670
It’s the largest structure astronomers have
discovered to date in the Universe—and they

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:32.440
don’t have any solid theories yet on why
exactly it’s so empty.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:38.340
It’s so extraordinary that even quantum
entanglement with another universe has been

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:39.340
considered.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:44.080
But until the Doctor drops by in his TARDIS
to let us know that we’re on the right track—or

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:49.050
at least until we gather more data—the Supervoid
will remain a mystery.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:54.570
You’re likely already familiar with the
concept of black holes: ominous dark maws

00:09:54.570 --> 00:10:00.490
in space, consuming anything that gets caught
in their gravitational pull—chewing up matter

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:05.080
and even light itself as though they were
little more than an appetizer before the main

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:06.080
course.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:09.540
There’s a lot we don’t know about black
holes—like what happens after something

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.320
is sucked beyond the event horizon—but if
there’s one thing we know for certain, it’s

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.860
that black holes are equal parts fascinating
and terrifying.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:22.870
But even more unnerving is that some of them
are known to “wander” aimlessly around

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:26.850
space chewing up everything in their path.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:30.260
Such entities are known as “Wandering Black
Holes”.

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:37.110
A huge, wandering black hole was discovered
in 2012 by NASA’s Chandra X-ray Observatory,

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:42.260
thanks to the efforts of a research team led
by Harvard-Smithsonian scientist Francesca

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:43.260
Civano.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:49.030
They found it in the vicinity of a galaxy
known as CID-42—of no relation to this channel,

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:53.060
of course—situated four billion light years
away from Earth.

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:57.240
It attracted the team’s attention due to
their observation that it was moving rather

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:02.730
quickly for an object containing many millions
of solar masses—clipping along at a speed

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:08.200
of no less than 4,820,000 kilometers per hour.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:13.690
Any planets in its way are going to end up
the galactic equivalent of a fly on a windshield—perhaps

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:16.340
we’re lucky it’s so far away.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:21.630
But there is in fact a “rogue” black hole
in the Milky Way galaxy, measured on two separate

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:26.770
occasions in 1996 and 2001 by the Hubble Space
Telescope.

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:32.250
Thanks to a star accompanying it along its
trajectory and the movement of the surrounding

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:37.380
stars, scientists were able to track it and
determine that it was moving four times faster

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.090
than anything nearby.

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:45.050
Speculation about its origin has led to multiple
theories, one being that it was shot out of

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:50.170
a supernova by the tremendous explosion of
a dying star, and the other concerning two

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:56.390
smaller galaxies which collided, ultimately
merging the black holes at their hearts, together.

00:11:56.390 --> 00:12:00.200
Regardless of how it came to be, there is
little that can stop it now.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:04.030
Just remember to look both ways when crossing
the interstellar highway.

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:08.010
While we’re on the topic of black holes,
at the center of our galaxy is a theorized

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:11.580
black hole so big that Muse wrote a song about
it.

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:14.640
And it is indeed “supermassive”.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.990
The distance from our humble home in a spiral
arm of the galaxy to the Milky Way’s galactic

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:23.330
core is just over eight kiloparsecs.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:28.450
For perspective, that’s more than two hundred
and forty-six quadrillion kilometers - though

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:33.750
the exact distance is difficult to measure
accurately due to the same “zone of avoidance”

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.920
that confounds scientists studying the Great
Attractor.

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:41.710
From our perspective here on Earth, the Galactic
Core is a densely packed nest of “globular

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:45.020
clusters”, each one a group of many thousands
of stars.

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:50.050
Imagine a ball of popcorn and you’re pretty
close to what it would look like.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:54.970
In the early 20th century, the very same Harlow
Shapely for whom the Shapely Supercluster

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:59.530
was named after, began trying to discover
the distances of different globular clusters

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:01.440
from our solar system.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:06.550
He soon found a notable collection of them
in the direction of the constellation Sagittarius,

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:11.650
and theorized that such a massive assembly
of them might be gathered at the galaxy’s

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:12.670
heart.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:18.230
His work led scientists to determine decades
later that he had in fact found the Galactic

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:19.230
Core.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:24.470
And just like the classic Joseph Conrad novel,
we also discovered that our galaxy has its

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:28.030
very own dark heart nestled deep within.

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:34.360
The Sagittarius A* supermassive black hole
can’t be detected directly via visible or

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:39.860
infrared light, so instead we infer its existence
due to the radio waves it gives off.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:44.820
They provide scientists with sufficient data
to calculate its mass—and it’s a big one,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:47.900
more than four million times bigger than our
sun.

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:51.700
At least it knows to stay put, instead of
careening across the universe like it’s

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:54.070
a free matter and light buffet.

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:59.490
In a universe that seems to be expanding in
all directions, it’s surprising to find

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:02.370
something out there that’s travelling straight
for us.

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:07.541
The Andromeda Galaxy, the closest large galaxy
to our own, is currently approaching us at

00:14:07.541 --> 00:14:11.190
a rate of around 400,000 kilometers per hour.

00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:16.779
But don’t worry just yet—it still has
another 2.5 million light years to travel,

00:14:16.779 --> 00:14:21.779
which puts it on track to reach the Milky
Way in around four billion years.

00:14:21.779 --> 00:14:26.620
Astronomers with NASA speculate that the collision
will result in a merging of the galaxies—which

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:32.160
could seriously shake up the neighborhood,
pushing our sun into a new section of the

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:33.160
galaxy.

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:37.980
They speculate that Earth and the other planets
in our solar system will continue orbiting

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:42.279
our sun after the mix-up, but of course, such
an event is unprecedented.

00:14:42.279 --> 00:14:45.310
There’s not a lot of data to work with.

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:49.960
On the initial approach, the galaxies will
most likely skirt each other without full

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:56.440
contact, narrowly missing before they spin
back around and slam into one another.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.800
Another theory suggests that after the merger,
our solar system might become a part of the

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:06.370
Andromeda galaxy afterward, looking back on
the spiral arms of our old galaxy in the night

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:07.370
sky.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:08.370
Quality fodder for science-fiction fans today—the
science-fact of tomorrow.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:11.740
This will all be happening in slow-motion;
very, very slow-motion.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:18.720
It’s expected that two billion years will
elapse between the near miss and the final

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:23.200
collision, so there’s plenty of time for
the futuristic Earthlings to admire the Andromeda

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:26.470
Galaxy taking up nearly all of the night sky
on the first go-around.

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:32.300
There’s even a slight chance that a satellite
galaxy of Andromeda’s could reach us first—but

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:33.690
hey, the more the merrier!

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:38.800
In a universe filled with rogue black holes
and colliding galaxies, it’s nice to find

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:42.880
something that’s pretty to look at without
posing a risk to our well-being.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:48.190
In the Serpens constellation, around 5,000
light years from Earth, astronomers were baffled

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:51.480
to discover a nebula with a very distinctive
shape.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:56.130
Thanks to the human mind’s tendency to find
familiar images in random patterns—known

00:15:56.130 --> 00:16:01.910
as Pareidolia—many nebulas have been named
for their uncanny similarity to objects we

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:05.730
know, like the Horse Head nebula and Cat’s
Eye Nebula.

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:11.290
But there’s no room for creative interpretation
here; the Red Square nebula of the Serpens

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:16.060
constellation is—well, a giant red square
among the stars.

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:22.430
The Red Square nebula is certainly eye-catching—appearing
to be an almost perfect square at first glance.

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:27.480
It’s actually formed by two cone-shaped
nebulae, which meet at the tips, and the mind

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:30.080
simply fills in the blanks to complete the
square.

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:34.611
But the sharp angles and lines that define
it grant it the honor of being “the most

00:16:34.611 --> 00:16:57.240
symmetrical object of comparable complexity”
found among the stars to date.

